# Chapter Two — The Guest List

= Draft: dramatic conversation cut 0.2. Director vibe pass: the company is worth remaining with. No interaction branches yet; wording and mystery setup remain revisable.

## Scene 3 — The Winter Room, arrivals

INT. THE WINTER ROOM, TOLLAND'S - EVENING

Present at opening: Henry Calder, Ruth Ellery, Lydia Shaw, Edwin Price.

= Immediate pressure: Lydia must assemble people who know one another too well without admitting that the guest list itself is an act of interference.

The elevator arrives. Its doors open on an empty vestibule.

For a moment nobody enters.

Then Marian Holt steps around the corner, pulling one glove loose finger by finger. She is a handsome woman in her middle fifties, dressed for a better restaurant than the one she remembers. When she sees Lydia, her face changes before she can decide what to change it into.

Lydia crosses to her.

They kiss once, lightly. Marian's ungloved hand remains on Lydia's arm. Lydia puts her hand over it. The gesture is old enough to occur without permission.

MARIAN
You look well.

LYDIA
I am leaving in the morning.

MARIAN
Those things can both be true.

LYDIA
That is why I invited you.

Marian removes her hand.

Edwin comes forward for her coat.

MARIAN
How is your knee?

EDWIN
It remains mine.

MARIAN
You said that at Easter.

EDWIN
The situation has not changed.

She smiles and gives him the coat. Henry does not yet know how they know one another.

Marian sees Ruth.

MARIAN
Ruth.

RUTH
Hello, Marian.

They embrace. Marian holds on a moment longer than Ruth expected. Ruth accepts the extra moment, then steps back.

MARIAN
I wasn't sure you would come.

RUTH
Neither was I.

MARIAN
Did she write to you?

RUTH
She called.

Marian looks at Lydia.

MARIAN
You called her.

LYDIA
She has a telephone.

MARIAN
You have had her address for six years.

RUTH
Seven.

MARIAN
Has it been?

RUTH
Only to me.

Marian touches Ruth's sleeve. Ruth lets her.

Marian turns to Henry.

MARIAN
You brought someone.

RUTH
Henry Calder. Marian Holt.

HENRY
Mrs. Holt.

MARIAN
Marian, please.

Marian looks back to Henry.

MARIAN
How do you know Ruth?

RUTH
He doesn't. Not properly.

HENRY
We met during a case.

MARIAN
Are you a lawyer?

RUTH
No. He listens for money.

MARIAN
That sounds worse.

HENRY
It often does.

Lydia returns to the table. One place card lies at a slant from the others. She straightens it without looking down.

MARIAN
You said seven.

LYDIA
Ruth was permitted one person.

MARIAN
By whom?

EDWIN
By me.

MARIAN
All right.

She walks to the windows. The city below is clear enough to resemble a promise. Edwin looks at his watch and goes out through the kitchen.

MARIAN
Robert said he would meet me here.

LYDIA
Did he?

MARIAN
At breakfast.

LYDIA
He may still.

Before Marian can ask again, Edwin comes through the kitchen door with Robert Holt. Robert is taking off his tie and explaining something about the entrance. He is broad through the shoulders and grayer than his wife.

ROBERT
—sent me back to Hill, but the Hill Street door was locked. I went around twice. Edwin found me arguing with the night guard.

MARIAN
You could have come with me.

ROBERT
I meant to.

MARIAN
You said you would meet me here.

ROBERT
I am here.

MARIAN
Yes, Robert.

He kisses Marian on the cheek. She permits the kiss but does not help him with it.

ROBERT
I'm sorry. I should have come with you.

MARIAN
Yes.

Robert waits, having expected another sentence. Marian has none.

He crosses to Lydia. She kisses his cheek and smooths the place where his tie had pressed into his collar.

ROBERT
There really is a train tomorrow?

LYDIA
In the morning.

ROBERT
I didn't know whether to believe the time.

LYDIA
Why would I invent a train time?

ROBERT
It made the story sound finished.

Lydia looks at him, then turns to the table.

Ruth moves closer to Henry without appearing to.

RUTH
You can still leave.

HENRY
Can you?

RUTH
That wasn't the offer.

Robert pours a drink. Before tasting it, he brings Marian a glass of water. She takes it without interrupting her conversation with Ruth.

Lydia introduces Henry.

LYDIA
Robert Holt. Henry Calder came with Ruth.

He offers Henry his hand.

ROBERT
How long have you known her?

HENRY
Long enough to be invited. Not long enough to know why.

ROBERT
That's most of us.

RUTH
Robert has known Lydia thirty years.

ROBERT
Twenty-eight.

MARIAN
Thirty. You met her before you met me.

ROBERT
I knew of her.

MARIAN
That counted when you were telling the story to my mother.

ROBERT
Your mother liked a complete story.

MARIAN
She liked the end first.

Lydia laughs. Marian tries not to, then does. Robert raises his glass to the absent woman.

The elevator bell interrupts their laughter.

Nell Avery enters with her coat folded over one arm. A faint line crosses her cheek where she had rested her hand.

NELL
She did like the end first.

MARIAN
You weren't there.

NELL
I've heard it every Christmas since I was twelve.

Marian smiles. Edwin crosses to take Nell's coat.

NELL
Hello, Edwin.

EDWIN
Miss Avery.

She gives him her coat, then puts her arms around him. Edwin receives the embrace with grave surprise and returns it carefully.

NELL
Thank you for tonight.

EDWIN
You may wait until it is over.

Nell goes directly to Lydia. Lydia opens her arms, and Nell embraces her.

Lydia closes her eyes.

NELL
You really are going.

LYDIA
Yes.

NELL
All right.

LYDIA
No, it isn't.

They separate. Lydia touches the ends of Nell's hair.

LYDIA
You cut it.

NELL
In June.

LYDIA
I missed it.

NELL
Yes.

Nell fixes Lydia's collar.

NELL
There.

LYDIA
Thank you.

Marian takes Nell's hand and squeezes it. Nell leans against her for a moment, then notices Henry.

NELL
Who is this?

RUTH
Henry. He came with me.

NELL
Are you staying for dinner?

HENRY
That was the arrangement.

NELL
Good.

She gives him her hand, then turns to Robert.

ROBERT
Did you eat anything today?

NELL
Breakfast.

ROBERT
Nell, it's after eight.

NELL
I know what time it is.

MARIAN
There are rolls.

NELL
Then let's sit down.

Only then does Nell look at Ruth.

NELL
Hello, Ruth.

RUTH
Hello.

Edwin brings a tray of small drinks from the service bar. Nobody has requested the same thing, yet each glass finds the person who expected it. Nell's is soda water with lime. She takes it, then looks at Lydia.

NELL
Thank you.

Lydia returns to the place cards.

LYDIA
We should sit.

MARIAN
Someone is missing.

LYDIA
Someone is always missing. It has never stopped dinner.

MARIAN
There are eight places.

RUTH
Henry made eight.

Nell looks at the cards. Seven names face outward. The eighth is turned toward Lydia.

NELL
Then why is there another card?

Nobody answers quickly enough.

Ruth reaches for it. Lydia places two fingers on the card before Ruth can turn it.

RUTH
Who else did you ask?

LYDIA
Someone who had not answered.

RUTH
You didn't tell me.

LYDIA
No.

RUTH
Why?

LYDIA
Would you have come?

Ruth turns the card over.

!PAUL ELLERY.

The elevator bell sounds once.

Nell puts down her drink. Robert looks at the floor.

The elevator doors open.

Paul Ellery remains in the vestibule. He is younger than Ruth by only a few years, but in the instant before he recognizes the room he looks much younger than that. Then he sees Ruth. Then Nell.

PAUL
You didn't say Ruth was coming.

LYDIA
I didn't tell Ruth you were coming.

RUTH
Stop helping.

PAUL
You said you hadn't told anyone.

LYDIA
I hadn't. You called at six and said yes, and I wrote the card.

MARIAN
Lydia.

Paul takes one step backward toward the elevator.

RUTH
Don't.

The doors begin to close. Henry catches one with his hand. It opens again.

Paul looks at him.

PAUL
Who are you?

HENRY
Dinner guest.

PAUL
This was a mistake.

RUTH
Yes. Stay anyway.

Paul looks at Nell. Nell rests both hands on the table.

NELL
Stay.

PAUL
Do you want me to?

NELL
No. But if you leave, the rest of them will spend the evening deciding what it meant. I'm hungry.

Paul looks at her. The corner of his mouth moves, then stops.

PAUL
All right.

Henry releases the elevator door. It closes behind Paul.

Edwin takes Paul's coat, then gathers the place cards from the table.

LYDIA
Edwin.

EDWIN
The first course is ready.

LYDIA
Those are the seats.

EDWIN
They were.

He slips the cards into his jacket pocket.

Nell chooses the chair across from Paul. Marian sits beside Nell. Robert sits beside Marian after a moment's hesitation. Ruth takes Henry's left side, and Lydia takes his right. Edwin remains standing nearest the kitchen.

## Scene 4 — The first course

= Present: Henry, Ruth, Lydia, Edwin, Marian, Robert, Nell, Paul.

= Immediate pressure: The guests must decide whether the evening can still be a dinner rather than an intervention performed upon Nell and Paul.

INT. THE WINTER ROOM - EVENING

The retained waiter enters from the kitchen with eight bowls. He reads the room well enough not to ask what has happened. Edwin helps him serve, then sends him back through the swinging door with a quiet word.

The soup is pale and gives off almost no steam.

ROBERT
Is it meant to be cold?

EDWIN
Yes.

ROBERT
I wanted to ask before Marian did.

MARIAN
I wasn't going to ask.

ROBERT
You looked at it.

MARIAN
It's soup. I looked.

Nell has already eaten several spoonfuls.

NELL
What is it?

EDWIN
Cucumber.

NELL
It's good.

Robert tastes it and nods. Edwin sits at last.

For several moments they eat. Marian takes the dish of salt, uses none, and sets it beside Robert before he asks. Ruth tears a roll in half and places one piece on Paul's bread plate. She realizes what she has done only after her hand is empty. Paul notices and says nothing.

Nell eats quickly. Lydia watches her, then deliberately looks away.

Edwin puts down his spoon and lifts his glass.

EDWIN
Miss Shaw requested no speeches. This is a toast.

LYDIA
Briefly.

He waits until the glasses rise.

EDWIN
Lydia has worked in this building for twenty-six years. That is not why we are here. We are here because she asked us. Tomorrow she says she is leaving. Tonight she is not.

He looks at Lydia.

EDWIN
To tonight.

MARIAN
To tonight.

The others repeat it. Henry does too.

Lydia drinks. Her eyes shine briefly, but she does not lower them.

ROBERT
And Portland.

LYDIA
Portland does not need encouragement.

NELL
Have you been there?

LYDIA
No.

NELL
Do you know anyone there?

LYDIA
No.

MARIAN
Do you have somewhere to live?

LYDIA
Yes.

ROBERT
Is there furniture?

LYDIA
Robert.

ROBERT
It matters after the first night.

LYDIA
There is furniture.

PAUL
What color is the front door?

Everyone looks at him.

PAUL
If we're establishing the place exists.

Lydia considers.

LYDIA
Blue.

PAUL
Dark or light?

LYDIA
Dark.

PAUL
House or apartment?

LYDIA
House.

PAUL
Whose house?

The room stills without becoming silent.

Lydia smiles at him.

LYDIA
Mine, beginning Thursday.

Marian leans back.

NELL
You bought a house.

LYDIA
I rented one.

NELL
Without seeing it.

LYDIA
The agent sent photographs.

MARIAN
Photographs.

ROBERT
We rented a room from photographs.

MARIAN
You rented it. I told you not to.

ROBERT
You came.

MARIAN
I had married you.

ROBERT
There were two windows in the picture.

NELL
One was painted on the wall.

Robert looks at her.

ROBERT
You've heard this.

NELL
Every Christmas since I was twelve.

PAUL
The painted one had the better view.

LYDIA
I lent them thirty dollars for curtains.

ROBERT
The real window faced a brick wall.

MARIAN
In Burbank.

ROBERT
Pasadena.

NELL
Burbank.

ROBERT
The Pasadena end of Burbank.

Marian laughs first. Lydia joins her. Then Nell and Paul, who have heard the story far too many times. Ruth laughs because Robert is still trying to move the room.

Nell reaches for the bread and finds the basket empty. Paul has the last roll on his plate. He looks at it, then places it in the basket and passes it across the table.

Nell breaks it in half and sends one half back.

Paul takes it.

For the next few minutes the soup is good, the room is warm, and the city below belongs to other people.

Henry looks at Ruth. She is laughing too.

She catches him looking.

RUTH
What?

HENRY
Nothing.

RUTH
That is what you say when something has become your business.

HENRY
I'm eating soup.

RUTH
Then try to look less employed.

Henry takes another spoonful.

For a little while, he does.
